name: scripts
boss: SIGEXIT
flavor: Whatever you started, I run last.
brief: Everything so far was a line you typed. A script is the same shell
brief: reading the lines instead of you -- which means it needs three things
brief: a prompt never does: arguments, structure, and a way to clean up.
brief:
brief: A function is structure. name() { ... } defines it, the bare name runs
brief: it. local keeps a variable inside it. return ends it with an exit
brief: status, the way exit ends the whole script.
brief:
brief: Arguments arrive as $1 $2 $3. shift drops $1 and slides the rest down,
brief: which is how you walk a list you did not write. getopts walks option
brief: letters instead, one call per option.
brief:
brief: trap is the cleanup. trap CMD EXIT runs CMD however the shell ends --
brief: normally, by error, or killed -- so a temp file you made gets removed
brief: on the path you did not plan for.
brief:
brief: exec and . both act on the shell you are in rather than a child: exec
brief: replaces it or rewires its file descriptors for good, . runs a script
brief: here so what it sets survives. Neither is undoable mid-line.
---
q: define a function f that echoes ok, then run it
a: f() { echo ok; }; f
cmp: ref
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the braces hold the body, and the bare name on its own calls it
---
q: define f to put 1 in v with local and echo v, then run f
a: f() { local v=1; echo $v; }; f
cmp: ref
ref: local
teach: local
hint: local keeps v inside f, so the caller never sees it change
---
q: define f to return 3, run it, then echo the exit status it left
a: f() { return 3; }; f; echo $?
cmp: ref
ref: return
teach: return
hint: return ends the function with a status, the way exit ends a script
---
q: set -- a b c, drop the first with shift, then echo the new first
a: set -- a b c; shift; echo $1
cmp: ref
ref: shift
teach: shift
hint: shift slides every parameter down one, which is how you walk a list
---
q: set -- a b c, shift by two, then echo what is left in first place
a: set -- a b c; shift 2; echo $1
cmp: ref
ref: shift
teach: shift
hint: a count shifts that many at once
---
q: set -- -a x, read one option letter with getopts into o, then echo o
a: set -- -a x; getopts a o; echo $o
cmp: ref
ref: getopts
teach: getopts
hint: the letters you declare are the ones it accepts, one call per option
---
q: run @script@ in this shell with dot, so it is not a child
a: . ./@script@
cmp: ref
ref: .
teach: .
hint: a dot-run script can change this shell, and a child could not
---
q: empty f with the null command, then count its bytes with wc
a: : > f; wc -c < f
cmp: ref
ref: :
teach: :
hint: the null command does nothing and succeeds, so the redirect is the point
---
q: trap EXIT to echo bye, then echo work
a: trap 'echo bye' EXIT; echo work
cmp: ref
ref: trap
teach: trap
hint: EXIT fires however the shell ends, which is where cleanup belongs
---
q: send errors to stdout with exec, then ls the missing file nosuch
a: exec 2>&1; ls nosuch
cmp: ref
ref: exec
teach: exec
hint: exec with only redirections rewires this shell instead of replacing it
---
q: replace this shell with echo done, using exec
a: exec echo done
cmp: ref
ref: exec
teach: exec
hint: with a command, exec becomes it -- nothing after the line ever runs
---
q: put the text echo hi in c, then run what it holds with eval
a: c="echo hi"; eval $c
cmp: ref
ref: eval
teach: eval
hint: eval expands the line a second time, then runs the result
---
q: pin r to 1 with readonly, then try to assign 2 to it
a: readonly r=1; r=2
status: 2
cmp: ref
ref: readonly
teach: readonly
hint: a readonly name cannot be reassigned, and the attempt ends the shell
---
q: use let to work out 1 + 2 into x, then echo x
a: let x=1+2; echo $x
cmp: ref
ref: let
teach: let
hint: let does arithmetic without $(( )) around it
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: v=1; f() { local v=2; }; f; echo $v
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: local keeps the assignment inside f -- the caller's v was never touched
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: set -- a b; shift 5; echo $#
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: shift past the end of the list is a no-op, not an error -- $# is left exactly as it was
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: f() { return 1; }; f; echo after
a: after
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: return only ends the function -- the caller keeps running right after the call
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: f() { return 7; }; f; echo $?
a: 7
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: return's argument becomes the function's exit status, same as $? after any command
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: trap 'echo bye' EXIT
a: bye
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: EXIT fires when the shell ends for any reason, including just running off the last line
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: x='$y'; y=hi; eval echo $x
a: hi
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: the line expands $x to the literal text $y first -- eval then expands THAT, reaching y's value
